          <figcaption>A study wheel on a laptop, with flashcards and a timer nearby.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2 id="formats">Five ways to load the wheel</h2>
        <p>The same wheel can answer different questions depending on what you put on it.</p>

        <h3>1. The topic wheel</h3>
        <p>
          Your specification topics, one per entry. Use it to choose the next block. This is the
          default and the one to start with.
        </p>

        <h3>2. The task wheel</h3>
        <p>
          Study methods instead of topics: blank-page recall, past-paper question, teach it aloud,
          make five flashcards, draw a diagram. Spin both wheels together and you get a topic and
          a method, which keeps sessions varied and pushes you toward active techniques.
        </p>

        <h3>3. The past-question wheel</h3>
        <p>
          Number your past-paper questions and let a wheel choose one blind. A
          <a href="/number-picker-wheel">number picker wheel</a> or the
          <a href="/randomizer-number-1-100">1 to 100 randomizer</a> handles this without
          typing every question in. Picking blind is closer to the exam, where you do not get to
          choose.
        </p>

        <h3>4. The weighted wheel</h3>
        <p>
          Add a weak topic two or three times so it comes up more often. This is a rough way to
          prioritise when you do not have time to plan. If you are not sure which topics are weak,
          write a quick score out of five next to each after a practice paper and add the lowest
          ones twice.
        </p>

        <h3>5. The break wheel</h3>
        <p>
          A short list of five-minute breaks: stretch, refill water, step outside, tidy your desk.
          Spinning for a break keeps it short and keeps your phone out of it. If you are torn
          between two topics, a <a href="/coin-flip-wheel">coin flip wheel</a> settles it in a
          second.
        </p>

        <h2 id="subjects">Ideas by subject</h2>
        <p>
          Load the wheel to match how the subject is actually examined. Here are starting points.
        </p>

        <h3>Maths and statistics</h3>
        <p>
          Put question types on the wheel (simultaneous equations, vectors, probability trees) and
          do one problem of each type before repeating any. Use a
          <a href="/number-picker-wheel">number wheel</a> to pick which exercise number to
          attempt so you cannot choose only the easy ones.
        </p>

        <h3>Sciences</h3>
        <p>
          For Biology, list processes; for Chemistry, reaction types; for Physics, calculation
          categories. Spin, then sketch or derive it from memory before opening notes. For fun
          context-switching, our <a href="/animal-picker-wheel">animal picker wheel</a> is handy
          for classification and adaptation practice, and the
          <a href="/planet-picker-wheel">planet picker wheel</a> suits astronomy units.
        </p>

        <h3>English and literature</h3>
        <p>
          Load your set texts, then separately load themes, characters and techniques. Spin one
          from each and write a timed paragraph linking them. A
          <a href="/letter-picker-wheel">letter picker wheel</a> also works as a vocabulary
          warm-up: spin a letter and write ten useful analytical words that start with it.
        </p>

        <h3>History and social sciences</h3>
        <p>
          Put events, figures or case studies on the wheel and give yourself 60 seconds to explain
          significance, causes and consequences out loud. Then load essay questions and plan an
          answer in five minutes without notes.
        </p>

        <h3>Languages</h3>
        <p>
          Load vocabulary themes, verb tenses or speaking prompts. Our guide to
          <a href="/blogs/spin-wheel-language-learning-vocabulary-verbs-conversation">spin wheels for language learning</a>
          has detailed setups, and the <a href="/language-picker-wheel">language picker wheel</a>
          is useful if you study more than one language and want to rotate between them.
        </p>

        <h3>Geography</h3>
        <p>
          Put case studies, key terms and data-skill types (reading a climate graph, describing a
          map) on the wheel. The <a href="/country-picker-wheel">country picker wheel</a>,
          <a href="/random-flag-generator">random flag generator</a> and
          <a href="/guess-the-country">guess the country game</a> make good warm-ups for
          place knowledge.
        </p>

        <h2 id="groups">Using it in a study group</h2>
        <p>
          In a group, the wheel also solves a social problem: nobody has to be the person who
          decides. A visibly random result removes arguments about whose weak topic comes first.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Teach-back:</strong> everyone lists one topic they feel shaky on; the wheel
            picks whose topic is explained first. Teaching is a strong test of what you actually
            understand.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Who goes next:</strong> use a <a href="/random-name-generator">random name generator</a>
            or a name wheel to pick who answers or presents, so everyone prepares for every
            question.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Pairs and teams:</strong> the <a href="/team-picker-wheel">team picker wheel</a>
            splits a larger group into quiz teams without friendships deciding it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Debate sides:</strong> for essay subjects, spin to assign for and against, then
            swap. Arguing a position you did not choose exposes weak points in your own.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Teachers can use the same approach for whole classes; see our guides to
          <a href="/blogs/spin-wheel-classroom-activities">classroom wheel activities</a>,
          <a href="/blogs/how-to-use-spin-wheel-classroom-examples">classroom setups with examples</a>
          and <a href="/blogs/name-picker-wheel-work-school">name picker wheels for work and school</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Re-spinning.</strong> The wheel only works if you accept the result. If you
            re-spin whenever you dislike it, you have rebuilt the negotiation you were trying to
            avoid.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Vague entries.</strong> "Chemistry" gives you no starting point. "Rates of
            reaction: collision theory" does.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Leaving hard topics off.</strong> This is the most common failure. The topics
            you avoid typing are the ones the wheel was meant to catch.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No task, no output.</strong> Spinning a topic and then rereading it is easy and
            low value. Decide the activity and the output first.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No time limit.</strong> Without a timer, one enjoyable topic eats the session
            and the rest never get their turn.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="compare">Wheel vs planner vs flashcard app</h2>
        <p>
          These tools are complementary rather than competing. A rough guide to when each fits:
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <caption>
              What each revision tool is best at
            </caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Tool</th>
                <th scope="col">Best for</th>
                <th scope="col">Weakness</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Spin wheel</th>
                <th scope="row" style="font-weight: 400">
                  Choosing what to do next; fair coverage; group sessions
                </th>
                <td>Does not know topic weight or your deadlines</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Timetable or planner</th>
                <th scope="row" style="font-weight: 400">
                  Allocating time against exam dates and topic weightings
                </th>
                <td>Takes effort to build; easy to abandon when it slips</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Flashcard app with spaced repetition</th>
                <th scope="row" style="font-weight: 400">
                  Memorising facts, definitions and vocabulary over weeks
                </th>
                <td>Less suited to essays, problem solving and big-picture topics</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          A sensible combination: use a planner to set the weekly plan, a flashcard app for facts,
          and a wheel to decide the order within each study session. For a comparison with other
          randomisers, see
          <a href="/blogs/spin-wheel-vs-other-random-pickers">spin wheels versus other random pickers</a>,
          or browse more uses in
          <a href="/blogs/best-uses-random-wheel-spinner">the best uses for a random wheel spinner</a>.
        </p>

