        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <div class="faq">
          <details>
            <summary>Is a spin wheel a good way to study?</summary>
            <p>
              It is good at one specific job: choosing what to work on next so you can start
              without deliberating. It does not teach anything by itself. The learning comes from
              what you do after the spin, so pair it with active methods such as self-testing and
              past-paper questions.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How many topics should I put on one study wheel?</summary>
            <p>
              Five to ten entries suits a single session or a single week. Each entry should be a
              topic you can make real progress on in one block of 25 to 45 minutes. For a large
              syllabus, build one wheel per week or per unit instead of one giant wheel.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Should I re-spin if I land on a topic I dislike?</summary>
            <p>
              No. Re-spinning brings back the negotiation the wheel is meant to remove. If a topic
              genuinely cannot be done right now, for example because you need a book you do not
              have, take it off the wheel before you start rather than after the spin.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Can I use a spin wheel for last-minute revision?</summary>
            <p>
              Yes. List your weakest topics more than once so they come up more often, and shorten
              each block to 20 or 25 minutes. A wheel then works as a quick, rough prioritisation
              tool when you do not have time to plan properly.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Is the wheel really random?</summary>
            <p>
              Each entry on a standard wheel has an equal chance on every spin, so results are
              independent. That means the same topic can come up twice in a row unless you remove
              entries after they are picked. Our explainer
              <a href="/blogs/is-spin-wheel-truly-random">is a spin wheel truly random?</a> covers
              the details.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How is a spin wheel different from a flashcard app?</summary>
            <p>
              Flashcard apps schedule reviews of individual facts. A spin wheel chooses between
              larger things: topics, question types or tasks. They solve different problems and
              work well together.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>

        <section class="takeaways" aria-labelledby="takeaways-title">
          <h2 id="takeaways-title">Key takeaways</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>A wheel makes starting easier by removing the first decision of a session.</li>
            <li>
              Write specific entries sized for one 25 to 45 minute block, and keep the wheel to
              five to ten items.
            </li>
            <li>Decide the study method and the output before you spin, and never re-spin.</li>
            <li>
              Pair the wheel with active recall and past papers; the wheel picks the order, not the
              method.
            </li>
            <li>
              Weight weak topics by listing them more than once, and use group wheels to keep
              sessions fair.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <p>
          For more tools you can load with your own lists, browse our
